1
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:12,400
Now when the ship had left the stream of the river
Ocean and come out into the swell of the wide-wayed sea

2
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,560
and the island of Aeaea — where the house of early-born

3
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:22,060
Dawn is, and her dancing-grounds, and the risings
of the sun — we ran her in there onto the sands

4
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:25,180
and stepped out ourselves onto the surf-line of the sea,

5
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:29,180
and there we dropped asleep, and waited for shining Dawn.

6
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:35,680
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
then I sent companions ahead to the house of Circe

7
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,980
to bring the body of Elpenor, who had died.

8
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:43,420
We cut billets of wood, quickly,
and where the headland ran out highest

9
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:47,740
we buried him, grieving, letting the big tears fall.

10
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:51,000
And when the dead man was burned,
and the dead man's armor,

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,120
we heaped a mound, and dragged a pillar-stone onto it,

12
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:57,820
and on the very top of the mound
we fixed his well-shaped oar.

13
00:00:57,820 --> 00:01:01,920
So we saw to it all, each thing.
And our coming up from Hades

14
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:07,840
was not lost on Circe: she readied herself and
came quickly, and her handmaids with her carried

15
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,060
bread and meat in plenty and fire-bright red wine.

16
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:16,360
And she stood in our midst and spoke,
shining among goddesses:

17
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:20,000
'Hard men — you who went down
living into the house of Hades:

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,140
twice-dying, when other men die once.

19
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:28,040
Come now: eat your food, and drink your wine,
here, all day long. And when dawn shows

20
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:34,560
you shall sail; and I will point out your road,
and make each thing plain, so that no grinding evil design,

21
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,780
on sea or on land, can catch you and make you suffer.'

22
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:41,860
So she spoke, and our proud hearts consented.

23
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:47,600
So then all day long till the setting of the sun we
sat feasting on meat past counting and sweet wine;

24
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:53,580
and when the sun went down and the dusk came on,
the men lay down to sleep beside the ship's stern-cables,

25
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,700
but she took me by the hand,
apart from my dear companions,

26
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:00,180
and made me sit, and lay beside me,
and asked me everything;

27
00:02:00,180 --> 00:02:03,360
and I told it all through to her, in its due order.

28
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:06,000
And then the lady Circe spoke to me in words:

29
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,800
'So all that has been brought to its end. Now hear

30
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:13,020
what I will tell you — and a god
himself will bring it back to your mind.

31
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:18,160
First you will come to the Sirens,
who enchant all mankind — whoever comes upon them.

32
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:23,280
Whoever in ignorance draws near and hears the
voice of the Sirens — no wife, no little children

33
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:26,300
will ever stand beside that man to welcome him home;

34
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:29,820
no — the Sirens enchant him with their clear-toned song,

35
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:32,780
sitting in a meadow; and around
them is a great heap of the bones

36
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:36,020
of rotting men, and the skins are shriveling on them.

37
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:38,440
Drive past them; and knead honey-sweet wax

38
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:41,680
and stop your companions' ears with it,
so that none of the others

39
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,720
may hear. But you yourself — listen, if you will:

40
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:47,580
only let them bind you in the fast ship, hand and foot,

41
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:51,580
upright in the mast-step, with the
rope-ends made fast to the mast itself,

42
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:54,940
so that you may hear the two
Sirens' voice, and delight in it.

43
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:57,780
And if you beg your companions —
if you order them to free you —

44
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,300
then let them bind you down with still more bonds.

45
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:05,220
But when your companions have driven past them,
from there I will not spell it out for you to the end —

46
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:10,420
which of two roads will be yours. You yourself must
weigh it in your heart. But I will tell you both.

47
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:15,600
On the one side, overhanging rocks; and against
them roars the great swell of dark-eyed Amphitrite.

48
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:18,660
The blessed gods call them the Wandering Rocks.

49
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:24,000
That way not even winged things pass — not even
the shy doves that carry ambrosia to father Zeus:

50
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,340
the sheer rock always takes one even of those,
and the Father sends in another to keep the number full.

51
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:34,940
That way no ship of men has ever yet escaped,
whichever came, but ships' planks and men's bodies together

52
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:39,400
are carried off by the waves of the
sea and the squalls of deadly fire.

53
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:46,700
One seagoing ship alone has sailed past there —
Argo, whom all minds know — sailing home from Aeetes;

54
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:50,120
and even her the wave would have
flung swiftly on the great rocks,

55
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:53,840
but Hera sent her through, because Jason was dear to her.

56
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:57,260
On the other side are two crags:
one reaches the wide heaven

57
00:03:57,260 --> 00:04:03,240
with its sharp peak, and a dark-blue cloud rings it,
never drawing off — no clear sky ever holds

58
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:06,720
that summit, not in summer, not in harvest-time.

59
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:12,160
No mortal man could climb it, or set foot on the top,
not if he had twenty hands and twenty feet:

60
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:15,400
the rock is smooth — as if dressed smooth all around.

61
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:20,720
And in the middle of the crag is a misty cave turned
toward the dark, toward Erebus — the very way you

62
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:23,660
will steer your hollow ship, shining Odysseus.

63
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:29,080
Not even a strong man's arrow, shot from a hollow ship,
could reach up into that vaulted cave.

64
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:31,780
And in it lives Scylla — yelping horribly.

65
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:39,040
Her voice, truly, is no more than a newborn puppy's,
but she herself is an evil monster: no one

66
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:43,640
could look at her and rejoice —
not a god, if a god should meet her.

67
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:49,180
Twelve feet she has, all of them dangling;
six necks, immensely long, and on each one

68
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:55,580
a hideous head, and in each three rows of teeth,
thick-set and crowded, full of black death.

69
00:04:55,580 --> 00:05:00,640
Waist-deep she is sunk in the hollow of the cave,
but she holds her heads out of that terrible pit

70
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:07,120
and fishes there, quivering with hunger around the crag,
for dolphins and dogfish, and whatever bigger beast

71
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:11,780
she can seize, of the thousands
deep-moaning Amphitrite pastures.

72
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:16,700
Past her no sailors yet can boast to have
fled unharmed with their ship: with each head

73
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:20,920
she carries off a man, snatched
from the dark-blue-prowed vessel.

74
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:27,360
The other crag is lower — you will see it, Odysseus —
close by the first: a bowshot would carry across.

75
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:34,240
On it stands a great fig tree, dense in leaf,
and beneath it shining Charybdis gulps down the black water.

76
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:38,020
Three times a day she spouts it forth,
three times she gulps it down —

77
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:41,480
terribly. May you not chance to be there when she gulps:

78
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:45,360
no one could save you from that evil
then — not the earth-shaker himself.

79
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:50,520
No: keep close to Scylla's crag,
and quickly drive the ship past. It is far better

80
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:53,880
to mourn six companions from your ship than all together.'

81
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:56,500
So she spoke, and I answered her and said:

82
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:59,540
'Come then, goddess — tell me this, and tell it true:

83
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:02,180
might I somehow slip out from under deadly Charybdis

84
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:06,360
and stand off the other,
when she strikes at my companions?'

85
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:10,200
So I spoke, and she answered at once,
shining among goddesses:

86
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:13,520
'Hard man! So even now your
heart is set on the works of war

87
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:17,080
and struggle. Will you not yield
even to the deathless gods?

88
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:22,880
She is not mortal — she is an immortal evil,
terrible, grim, savage, not to be fought.

89
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:25,600
There is no defense. To flee her is the best courage.

90
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:30,900
For if you linger by the rock to arm yourself,
I fear she will dart out a second time and catch you

91
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:34,100
with all six heads, and take six men again.

92
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:37,400
No — row past with all your force, and call on Crataiis,

93
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:40,980
the mother of Scylla, who bore her, a plague on mortals:

94
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:43,280
she will stop her then from darting out again.

95
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:46,340
And you will come to the island Thrinacia. There in plenty

96
00:06:46,340 --> 00:06:49,740
graze the cattle of Helios, and his strong flocks:

97
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:55,500
seven herds of cattle, and as many fine flocks of
sheep, fifty in each. No young are born to them,

98
00:06:55,500 --> 00:07:02,000
and they never die away. Goddesses are their shepherds,
nymphs of the lovely braids, Phaethusa and Lampetie,

99
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,960
whom shining Neaera bore to Helios Hyperion.

100
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:10,480
Their honored mother, when she had borne and reared them,
settled them far away, on the island Thrinacia,

101
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:13,200
to keep their father's sheep and his crook-horned cattle.

102
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:16,740
If you leave these unharmed,
and keep your mind on homecoming,

103
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:22,340
then you may yet reach Ithaca, though you suffer evils;
but if you harm them, then I foretell destruction

104
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:26,780
for ship and companions. And even if you yourself escape,

105
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:30,920
late you will come, and in evil case,
all companions lost.'

106
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:34,860
So she spoke; and at once Dawn
of the golden throne came on.

107
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:40,600
Then she went away up the island, shining among goddesses,
and I went to the ship and roused my companions

108
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:43,860
to board in their turn and loose the stern-cables;

109
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:47,560
and they came aboard at once and
took their places at the oarlocks,

110
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:51,040
and sitting in order they struck
the gray salt water with their oars.

111
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:57,300
And for us, behind the dark-blue-prowed ship,
a following wind that filled the sail, a good companion,

112
00:07:57,300 --> 00:08:01,460
was sent by Circe of the lovely braids,
dread goddess with a mortal's voice.

113
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:03,960
So when we had seen to the tackle through the ship,

114
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:07,380
we sat, and the wind and the steersman held her on course.

115
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:11,600
Then, grieving at heart, I spoke among my companions:

116
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:15,020
'Friends — it is not right that one man only, or two,

117
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:18,640
should know the decrees that Circe
told me, shining among goddesses.

118
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:25,840
No: I will speak, so that knowing them we may die —
or swerve aside, and escape death and doom.

119
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:31,320
First she commands us to shun the marvelous Sirens —
their voice, and their flowering meadow.

120
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:34,920
Me alone she bade listen to their song. But bind me

121
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:38,620
in a bond that bites, so that I stay fixed where I stand,

122
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:42,680
upright in the mast-step, with the
rope-ends made fast to the mast itself.

123
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:49,080
And if I beg you — if I order you to free me —
then squeeze me down with still more bonds.'

124
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:53,860
So I went through it all, telling my companions,
while the well-made ship ran swiftly on

125
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:57,520
toward the island of the two Sirens:
a fair wind drove her.

126
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:04,260
Then all at once the wind fell still, and a calm came,
breathless: some power lulled the waves to sleep.

127
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:09,280
My companions rose and furled the sails and stowed
them in the hollow ship, and sitting to the oars

128
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:12,440
they whitened the water with the polished blades of fir.

129
00:09:12,440 --> 00:09:15,400
And I with my sharp bronze cut a great round cake of wax

130
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:18,900
into little pieces, and kneaded them in my heavy hands;

131
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:22,580
and quickly the wax grew warm,
for my great strength pressed it,

132
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:28,860
and the beam of the lord Helios, Hyperion's son;
and one by one I stopped the ears of all my companions.

133
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:32,020
And they bound me in the ship, hand and foot together,

134
00:09:32,020 --> 00:09:35,880
upright in the mast-step, and made
the rope-ends fast to the mast itself;

135
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:39,700
and sitting down they struck the
gray salt water with their oars.

136
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:42,680
But when we were as far off as a man's shout carries,

137
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:45,980
running swiftly, the sea-quick ship did not slip past them

138
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:50,100
unseen as she leapt near —
and they raised their clear-toned song:

139
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:54,660
'Come here, come, much-praised Odysseus,
great glory of the Achaeans:

140
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:57,840
bring your ship in, so you may hear the voice of us two.

141
00:09:57,840 --> 00:10:01,260
For no man yet has rowed past this place in his black ship

142
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:03,940
before hearing the honey-voiced song from our lips —

143
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:07,880
no: he has his delight of it,
and goes his way knowing more.

144
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:13,140
For we know all that in wide Troy the Argives and
the Trojans labored through by the gods' will;

145
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:16,340
and we know all that comes to
pass on the nourishing earth.'

146
00:10:16,340 --> 00:10:19,820
So they sang, sending out their
beautiful voice; and my heart

147
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:23,440
longed to listen, and I ordered my companions to free me,

148
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:27,660
signaling with my brows; but they
threw themselves forward and rowed.

149
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:33,840
And at once Perimedes and Eurylochus rose and bound
me with more bonds, and squeezed them tighter.

150
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:39,260
But when they had driven past them, and we could
no longer hear the Sirens' voice, or their singing,

151
00:10:39,260 --> 00:10:44,920
quickly my trusted companions took out the wax I had
stopped their ears with, and loosed me from my bonds.

152
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:52,580
But when we had left the island behind, at once I
saw smoke, and a great wave, and heard a booming.

153
00:10:52,580 --> 00:10:55,660
The men were terrified: the oars flew from their hands

154
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:59,680
and clattered, all of them,
along the current; and the ship stopped

155
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:03,380
dead, since their hands no
longer drove the tapered blades.

156
00:11:03,380 --> 00:11:09,580
And I went through the ship, heartening my companions
with gentle words, standing by each man in turn:

157
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:13,600
'Friends — we are not unschooled in evils, not we.

158
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:17,220
This evil that stands over us now
is no greater than when the Cyclops

159
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:23,860
penned us in his hollow cave by brute force — and even
from there, by my prowess and my counsel and my mind,

160
00:11:23,860 --> 00:11:27,740
we escaped. Someday, I think,
we will remember this one too.

161
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:31,700
Come then — all of us — do exactly as I say.

162
00:11:31,700 --> 00:11:33,860
You, keep to your benches and strike the deep surf

163
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:40,140
of the salt sea with your oar-blades, in hope Zeus
somehow may grant us to slip clear of this destruction;

164
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:43,300
and you, steersman, here is my order — lay it up

165
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:47,020
in your heart, since you handle the
steering-oar of our hollow ship:

166
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:52,920
keep the ship outside that smoke and that surf,
and hug the crag, for fear the ship, before you know it,

167
00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:56,500
shears off toward the other side —
and you pitch us into the evil.'

168
00:11:56,500 --> 00:11:59,780
So I spoke, and they quickly heeded my words.

169
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:05,580
But Scylla I did not speak of — that hopeless horror —
for fear my companions, terrified, might drop

170
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:08,340
the oars and pack themselves into the hold.

171
00:12:08,340 --> 00:12:13,920
And then — Circe's hard command, that I should
not arm myself — I let it slip from my mind:

172
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:19,740
I put on my famous armor, took two long spears in
my hands, and went up to the half-deck at the bow,

173
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:25,240
for it was from there I expected the first sight of
Scylla of the rock, bringing ruin on my companions.

174
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:31,120
And I could not glimpse her anywhere; my eyes grew
weary scanning every hand-span of the misty rock.

175
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:33,640
So we sailed up the narrows, wailing:

176
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:39,800
on this side Scylla; on that, shining Charybdis
gulped down, terribly, the salt water of the sea.

177
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:43,380
When she vomited it up, like a cauldron over a great fire

178
00:12:43,380 --> 00:12:47,060
she would seethe and boil in all
her churning depth, and the spray

179
00:12:47,060 --> 00:12:52,520
fell high on the very tops of both the crags;
but when she gulped the salt sea down again,

180
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:55,620
all her churning depth showed open,
and the rock around her

181
00:12:55,620 --> 00:13:02,140
roared terribly, and at the bottom the earth showed,
dark-blue with sand. And the pale terror took them.

182
00:13:02,140 --> 00:13:07,740
We looked to her, then, in our dread of destruction —
and that was when Scylla snatched from the hollow ship

183
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:11,200
six of my companions, the best in hands and strength.

184
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:16,420
Turning to look at the fast ship, after my men,
I caught their feet and hands already above me,

185
00:13:16,420 --> 00:13:19,000
swung high aloft; and they cried out and called to me

186
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,500
by name — for the last time —
in the anguish of their hearts.

187
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:29,000
As when a fisherman on a jutting rock, with his long rod,
casts down baits as a snare for the little fishes,

188
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:35,240
dropping into the sea the horn of a field-ox,
and each fish he catches he flings up gasping onto the land:

189
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:38,660
so they were swung gasping up toward the rocks.

190
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:42,680
And there in her doorway she devoured them,
shrieking as they were,

191
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:45,800
stretching their hands toward me in the terrible struggle.

192
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:48,980
That was the most pitiful thing my eyes ever looked on

193
00:13:48,980 --> 00:13:52,200
of all I suffered searching out the pathways of the sea.

194
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:58,460
Now when we had escaped the rocks, and dread Charybdis
and Scylla, we came quickly then to the god's

195
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:01,620
blameless island: there were the beautiful broad-browed

196
00:14:01,620 --> 00:14:06,280
cattle, and the many strong flocks, of Helios Hyperion.

197
00:14:06,280 --> 00:14:11,620
And while I was still at sea, in the black ship,
I heard the lowing of cattle being folded

198
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:17,800
and the bleating of sheep; and there fell upon my
heart the word of the blind seer, Theban Tiresias,

199
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:24,980
and of Circe of Aeaea, who charged me over and over
to shun the island of Helios, the delight of mortals.

200
00:14:24,980 --> 00:14:28,780
Then, grieving at heart, I spoke among my companions:

201
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:34,700
'Hear my words, companions, for all your suffering,
so I may tell you the prophecies of Tiresias

202
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:41,920
and of Circe of Aeaea, who charged me over and over
to shun the island of Helios, the delight of mortals:

203
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:45,420
there, she said, is the most terrible evil waiting for us.

204
00:14:45,420 --> 00:14:48,800
No — drive the black ship on, wide of the island.'

205
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:51,800
So I spoke, and their own hearts broke within them.

206
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:56,000
And at once Eurylochus answered me — with a hateful word:

207
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,820
'You are a hard one, Odysseus.
Your strength is beyond us; your limbs

208
00:14:58,820 --> 00:15:02,700
never tire. Truly you are made
of iron through and through —

209
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:05,620
you who will not let your companions, worn down with labor

210
00:15:05,620 --> 00:15:08,820
and sleeplessness, set foot on land, where on this

211
00:15:08,820 --> 00:15:12,600
sea-ringed island we might at
last make ready a savory supper;

212
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:16,660
no — just as we are, through the
fast-falling night, you order us

213
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:20,620
to wander, driven off the island, over the misted sea.

214
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:26,540
Out of the nights are born the hard winds, the wreckers
of ships. How would a man escape sheer destruction

215
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:29,920
if somehow, suddenly, a squall of wind should come,

216
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:33,180
South Wind or hard-blowing West,
which more than any others

217
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:36,960
smash ships to pieces — in despite of the gods our lords?

218
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:42,700
No: for now let us yield to black night and
make our supper ready, staying by the fast ship;

219
00:15:42,700 --> 00:15:46,120
at dawn we will board, and stand out onto the wide sea.'

220
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:49,420
So spoke Eurylochus, and the other companions approved.

221
00:15:49,420 --> 00:15:55,920
And then I knew it: some power was plotting evils —
and lifting my voice I spoke winged words to him:

222
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:59,160
'Eurylochus, you force my hand: I am one man alone.

223
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:02,160
But come — every one of you swear me a strong oath:

224
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:07,280
if we find some herd of cattle or great flock
of sheep, let no man in his evil recklessness

225
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:12,680
kill one cow, one sheep. No: sit quiet
and eat the food immortal Circe gave.'

226
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:16,680
So I spoke, and they swore at once, as I demanded.

227
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:21,420
And when they had sworn, and made an end of the oath,
we moored the well-made ship in the hollow harbor

228
00:16:21,420 --> 00:16:27,600
close by a spring of sweet water, and the companions
went ashore, and skillfully made their supper ready.

229
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:30,320
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,

230
00:16:30,320 --> 00:16:33,000
they fell to weeping, remembering their dear companions

231
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:38,880
whom Scylla had snatched from the hollow ship and eaten;
and as they wept, a deep sleep came upon them.

232
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:42,940
But in the night's third watch,
when the stars had crossed their height,

233
00:16:42,940 --> 00:16:46,220
Zeus who gathers the clouds
roused a raging wind against us

234
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:52,600
in a howling squall, and covered land and sea
alike with cloud; and night rose up out of heaven.

235
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:55,800
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

236
00:16:55,800 --> 00:16:59,420
we dragged the ship up and made her fast in a hollow cave

237
00:16:59,420 --> 00:17:04,560
where the nymphs had their fine dancing-floors and seats;
and then I called them all together, and said:

238
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:07,600
'Friends — there is food and drink in the fast ship:

239
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:11,340
let us keep our hands off these cattle,
or we shall suffer for it.

240
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:14,560
These are the cattle and the
strong flocks of a terrible god —

241
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:17,820
Helios, who sees all things and hears all things.'

242
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:20,640
So I spoke, and their proud hearts consented.

243
00:17:20,640 --> 00:17:24,160
Then for a whole month the South
Wind blew without let, and no other

244
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:27,320
wind rose after that, except the East and the South.

245
00:17:27,320 --> 00:17:31,120
As long as the men had bread and red wine,
they kept their hands from the cattle:

246
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:36,840
But when all the ship's provisions were spent,
and need drove them roaming after game —

247
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:42,720
fish, birds, whatever came to their dear hands —
with bent hooks, while hunger wore their bellies down,

248
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:48,780
then I went away up the island, to pray to the gods,
in hope one might show me a road home.

249
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:52,440
And when, crossing the island,
I had left my companions behind,

250
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:57,720
I washed my hands where there was shelter from the
wind and prayed to all the gods who hold Olympus —

251
00:17:57,720 --> 00:18:00,700
and they poured sweet sleep upon my eyelids.

252
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:04,240
And Eurylochus began his evil counsel to the companions:

253
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:07,580
'Hear my words, companions, for all your suffering.

254
00:18:07,580 --> 00:18:10,080
All deaths are hateful to wretched mortals —

255
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:14,700
but to die of hunger, and meet
doom that way, is the most pitiful.

256
00:18:14,700 --> 00:18:18,140
Come then: let us drive off the
best of the cattle of Helios

257
00:18:18,140 --> 00:18:21,180
and sacrifice them to the immortals who hold wide heaven.

258
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:27,420
And if we come to Ithaca, our fathers' land,
we will build at once a rich temple to Helios Hyperion

259
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:29,980
and set up in it fine offerings, many of them.

260
00:18:29,980 --> 00:18:33,320
But if, in anger over his straight-horned cattle, he wills

261
00:18:33,320 --> 00:18:39,020
to destroy our ship, and the other gods go with him —
I would rather gulp once at the wave and lose my life

262
00:18:39,020 --> 00:18:42,480
than be starved slowly to death on a desert island.'

263
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:45,860
So spoke Eurylochus, and the other companions approved.

264
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:49,100
And at once they drove off the
best of the cattle of Helios,

265
00:18:49,100 --> 00:18:52,640
from close by — for not far from the dark-blue-prowed ship

266
00:18:52,640 --> 00:18:55,880
the beautiful broad-browed
crook-horned cattle were grazing —

267
00:18:55,880 --> 00:19:01,020
and they stood around them and prayed to the gods,
plucking the tender leaves of a high-crowned oak:

268
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:03,980
for they had no white barley aboard the well-benched ship.

269
00:19:03,980 --> 00:19:07,180
And when they had prayed, and cut the throats, and flayed,

270
00:19:07,180 --> 00:19:10,700
they carved out the thigh-pieces
and wrapped them deep in fat,

271
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:16,580
folding it double, and laid raw flesh upon them;
and they had no wine to pour over the blazing offerings,

272
00:19:16,580 --> 00:19:20,420
so they made libation with water,
and roasted all the entrails.

273
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:23,380
And when the thighs were burned through,
and they had tasted the innards,

274
00:19:23,380 --> 00:19:27,240
they carved up the rest and threaded it on spits.

275
00:19:27,240 --> 00:19:30,000
Then it was that the deep sleep fled from my eyelids,

276
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,420
and I went down to the fast ship and the shore of the sea.

277
00:19:33,420 --> 00:19:38,580
But as I came near the curved ship, the sweet
smell of the fat's smoke drifted all around me,

278
00:19:38,580 --> 00:19:42,240
and I groaned aloud, and cried out to the deathless gods:

279
00:19:42,240 --> 00:19:45,680
'Father Zeus, and you other blessed gods who are forever —

280
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:49,720
it was for my ruin, then, that you
laid me down in that pitiless sleep,

281
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:53,720
while my companions, waiting,
devised this monstrous thing.'

282
00:19:53,720 --> 00:19:56,940
Swiftly the word came to Helios Hyperion:

283
00:19:56,940 --> 00:20:00,420
long-robed Lampetie brought it —
that we had killed his cattle.

284
00:20:00,420 --> 00:20:04,760
And at once, with anger in his heart,
he spoke among the immortals:

285
00:20:04,760 --> 00:20:08,180
'Father Zeus, and you other blessed gods who are forever:

286
00:20:08,180 --> 00:20:11,840
make them pay — the companions of Odysseus, Laertes' son —

287
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:14,500
who in their insolence have killed my cattle: the cattle

288
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:18,360
I took my joy in, every day I
climbed toward the starry heaven

289
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:21,880
and every day I turned from heaven back toward earth.

290
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:28,140
If they do not pay me a fitting price for the cattle,
I will go down into Hades and shine among the dead.'

291
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:30,840
Then Zeus who gathers the clouds answered him:

292
00:20:30,840 --> 00:20:37,180
'Helios — you shine on among the immortals, surely,
and among mortal men across the grain-giving plowland;

293
00:20:37,180 --> 00:20:40,260
and as for those men: their fast ship I will soon strike

294
00:20:40,260 --> 00:20:44,940
with the white lightning, and shatter it to
splinters in the middle of the wine-dark sea.'

295
00:20:44,940 --> 00:20:48,000
— This I heard afterward from fair-haired Calypso;

296
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:53,980
and she said she heard it herself from Hermes the guide.
— Now when I had come down to the ship and the sea,

297
00:20:53,980 --> 00:20:57,860
I went up and down and rebuked them,
man by man; but no remedy

298
00:20:57,860 --> 00:21:01,120
could we find anywhere: the cattle were dead already.

299
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:04,660
And soon the gods began to show
forth portents among the men:

300
00:21:04,660 --> 00:21:07,720
the hides crawled; the meat on the spits was lowing,

301
00:21:07,720 --> 00:21:12,660
the roasted and the raw; and there
rose a sound as of cattle crying.

302
00:21:12,660 --> 00:21:15,120
Six days after that my trusted companions

303
00:21:15,120 --> 00:21:20,040
feasted on the cattle of Helios they
had driven off, the best of them;

304
00:21:20,040 --> 00:21:23,600
but when Zeus the son of Cronos
set the seventh day upon us,

305
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:30,560
then at last the wind fell from its raging squall,
and we boarded at once and stood out onto the wide sea,

306
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:33,520
stepping the mast and hauling up the white sails.

307
00:21:33,520 --> 00:21:39,160
But when we had left the island behind,
and no other land showed anywhere — only sky and sea —

308
00:21:39,160 --> 00:21:45,620
then the son of Cronos set a dark-blue cloud over
the hollow ship, and the deep went black beneath it.

309
00:21:45,620 --> 00:21:52,340
And she ran on not much longer: for all at once came
the shrieking West Wind, raging in a great squall,

310
00:21:52,340 --> 00:21:55,760
and the storm-blast snapped both forestays of the mast:

311
00:21:55,760 --> 00:21:58,640
the mast toppled backward, and all its tackle spilled

312
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:01,920
down into the bilge; and in the stern of the ship the mast

313
00:22:01,920 --> 00:22:07,600
struck the steersman's head, and crushed the bones of
the skull all together; and like a diver he dropped

314
00:22:07,600 --> 00:22:11,420
from the half-deck, and his proud life left his bones.

315
00:22:11,420 --> 00:22:15,540
And Zeus thundered in the same instant,
and hurled his bolt into the ship,

316
00:22:15,540 --> 00:22:19,640
and she shivered stem to stern,
struck by the bolt of Zeus,

317
00:22:19,640 --> 00:22:23,420
and filled with the reek of sulfur;
and the men fell from the ship.

318
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:25,240
Like sea-crows around the black hull

319
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:29,280
they were carried off on the waves;
and the god took away their homecoming.

320
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:31,940
But I kept moving through the ship, until the surge

321
00:22:31,940 --> 00:22:35,740
tore the sides loose from the keel,
and the wave carried her stripped bare,

322
00:22:35,740 --> 00:22:39,080
and snapped the mast off at the keel. But over the mast

323
00:22:39,080 --> 00:22:45,780
the backstay had been flung — a thing made of ox-hide;
with it I lashed the two together, keel and mast,

324
00:22:45,780 --> 00:22:49,840
and settling myself on them,
I was carried before the deadly winds.

325
00:22:49,840 --> 00:22:52,900
Then indeed the West Wind fell from its raging squall,

326
00:22:52,900 --> 00:22:57,020
but the South came fast in its place,
bringing anguish to my heart:

327
00:22:57,020 --> 00:23:00,200
that I should measure back my way to deadly Charybdis.

328
00:23:00,200 --> 00:23:05,020
All night I was carried; and with the rising sun
I came to Scylla's crag, and to dread Charybdis.

329
00:23:05,020 --> 00:23:11,600
She was gulping down the salt water of the sea —
but I, swinging up high toward the tall fig tree,

330
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:14,900
caught it and clung there like a bat. And there was no way

331
00:23:14,900 --> 00:23:18,240
to plant my feet firm anywhere, no way to climb it:

332
00:23:18,240 --> 00:23:24,220
the roots were far below, and the branches out of reach,
long and great boughs that shadowed Charybdis.

333
00:23:24,220 --> 00:23:27,180
I clung there without slackening,
until she should vomit back

334
00:23:27,180 --> 00:23:30,080
the mast and keel again. And to my longing, late,

335
00:23:30,080 --> 00:23:34,320
they came: at the hour when a man
rises from the assembly for supper —

336
00:23:34,320 --> 00:23:37,280
the judge of many quarrels among contending young men —

337
00:23:37,280 --> 00:23:40,500
at that hour the timbers reappeared out of Charybdis.

338
00:23:40,500 --> 00:23:46,660
I let go, feet and hands, from above, and dropped
with a crash into the water beyond the long timbers,

339
00:23:46,660 --> 00:23:49,540
and settling on them, I rowed hard with my hands.

340
00:23:49,540 --> 00:23:53,080
And Scylla — the father of men and gods did not let her

341
00:23:53,080 --> 00:23:57,000
see me again: I would not have
slipped clear of sheer destruction.

342
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:01,680
From there I was carried nine days;
and on the tenth, in the black night,

343
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:05,360
the gods brought me near the island Ogygia, where Calypso

344
00:24:05,360 --> 00:24:10,340
lives, she of the lovely braids,
the dread goddess with a mortal's voice,

345
00:24:10,340 --> 00:24:14,620
and she loved me and cared for me.
But why tell that tale over?

346
00:24:14,620 --> 00:24:20,540
Only yesterday I told it in this house —
to you and to your strong wife. And it is hateful to me

347
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:24,000
to tell a tale over again that has been told out plainly."
